00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.990
Leah: So, what are the strengths and
weaknesses of Waterfall and when should

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:07.080
it be used over other methodologies?

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.320
Chris: The Strengths are it’s easy to
use and manage -Waterfall is very easy

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.470
to use and learn as it is a linear
approach which runs from one stage to

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:19.580
the next until the project is complete.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:24.830
Each stage has certain deliverables and a
review so the next stage wouldn’t happen

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:26.840
until that stage has been signed off.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:31.570
Suitable for certain types of projects
that required a high degree of

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:36.679
predictability- As all the planning along
with strong supporting documentation

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:40.050
that occurs at the beginning of
a project, there is a high degree

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:44.220
of predictability which is more
suitable for large scale projects.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:48.889
Discipline is built in-Each
project has specific start

00:00:48.889 --> 00:00:50.449
and end points to the project.

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:54.629
It is at these points that the
client and stakeholders are involved.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:59.249
The project isn’t started until the
requirements and design has been

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:03.710
signed off, so the deadlines and
requirements are likely to be met.

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:09.500
Documentation-One of the key
principles of ‘Waterfall’ is its

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.885
strong supporting documentation
which is required for each stage.

00:01:12.885 --> 00:01:19.179
This supports the team and project,
less training requirements, supports

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:23.709
future projects and supplies details
to the stakeholders if needed.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.110
Leah: However, the Weaknesses are it’s

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:30.980
Difficult to change- As Waterfall is
a linear approach it does not have the

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:36.380
flexibility of Agile as once a stage is
complete, it is difficult to go back.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:42.490
Software delivered at end of production-As
the software/product is delivered at the

00:01:42.539 --> 00:01:47.279
end of the production there is a risk that
it won’t meet stakeholder’s expectations.

00:01:48.069 --> 00:01:53.119
Gathering accurate requirements can be
challenging-The first stage is crucial

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:57.319
and would need a Project Manager with
effective communication skills as this is

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:01.180
where the requirements from the customers
and stakeholders would be gathered.

00:02:01.759 --> 00:02:06.210
A customer may not know what they want
until later on in the project and this

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:10.340
could cause issues in the process and
have an impact on the whole project.

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:14.380
Large projects which have dependencies
on external systems such as

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:18.400
hardware, and where the requirements
need to remain fixed with little

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:22.359
tolerance for failure [or bugs]
would use a Waterfall methodology.

00:02:23.139 --> 00:02:27.150
Examples of this include ‘payment
gateways’, ‘military defence

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:32.780
systems’, ‘construction’, ‘flight
controls’, ‘manufacturing plants’ etc

